    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey so im studying for my materials class and im a bit confused. why do we actually need to put steel rebar inside concrete for foundations? i get that concrete is strong, but whats the deal with the relationship between its compressive strength and the tensile strength of the steel? like why do they need each other?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Concrete is very good at bearing compressive loads but its tensile capacity is only about 10 % of its compressive strength, so it cracks easily under tension. Steel rebars have a tensile strength that is roughly ten times higher than concrete’s tensile capacity and they bond well to the curing concrete, allowing the composite section to share loads: concrete carries most of the compressive stress while the steel resists the tensile stresses that develop in a foundation. This cooperative action also reduces cracking, limits deformation, and gives the member ductility, so the structure can survive overloads or settlement without sudden failure. In short, each material does what the other does poorly, creating a strong, flexible foundation.",
      "source": "target",
