      "role": "user",
      "content": "ok so im thinking about the \"internal\" part. since the steel is buried deep inside the solid concrete block, is it like the concrete is just a shell for the steel or does the steel have to be spaced out in a certain way so the whole \"internal\" structure stays stable?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The steel isn’t just a hidden core; it’s distributed throughout the concrete according to a design grid that matches the expected stress pattern. In a slab or footing, rebars are placed near the bottom (where tension occurs) and sometimes near the top (to resist cracking or to carry reverse‑bending), with spacing limited so that concrete between bars can develop enough bond and so cracks can’t open wider than a code‑prescribed maximum (usually 3–5 mm). This spacing also lets the concrete act as a protective “shell,” keeping the steel from corrosion and sharing compressive stresses. By arranging the bars in multiple layers and maintaining proper cover and spacing, the composite section behaves as a unified, stable unit rather than a steel core surrounded by a weak shell.",
